---
title: "Basic tutorial 4: Record trap lines as collecting events"
authors:
  - Heru Handika
lastUpdated: 2026-11-19
sidebar:
  label: "4. Record collecting events"
  order: 4
---

A site records *where*. A **collecting event** records *when, how, and by whom*:
the dates a line ran, the traps deployed, the people who worked it, and the
weather. Specimens link to an event rather than directly to a site, making the
event the bridge that gives each specimen its place, method, and collector.

On paper, these details are scattered: trap counts live in the journal, the
method is rewritten on every specimen sheet, and the teammates who checked traps
are never listed on the forms.

## 1. Plan the events

Each line ran once, so each site gets one event. All dates and trap counts below
come from the field journal—none appear on the specimen forms.

| Site | Start | End | Method | Count | Event notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `TJHH-L1` | 12 Sep 2014 | 16 Sep 2014 | `Snap trap` | 31 | One broken trap removed on 14 Sep. Rebaiting stopped on 15 Sep; some traps left baited. |
| `TJHH-P1` | 12 Sep 2014 | 16 Sep 2014 | `Pitfall` | 10 | About 50 m from camp, next to the Line 1 snap traps. |
| `TJBS-L2` | 12 Sep 2014 | 18 Sep 2014 | `Snap trap` | 25 | Most traps 1–2 m above the ground. |
| `TJHH-L3` | 16 Sep 2014 | 18 Sep 2014 | `Snap trap` | 20 | Checked by boat. |
| `TJHH-P4` | 16 Sep 2014 | 18 Sep 2014 | `Pitfall` | Leave empty | Number of pitfalls not recorded. Checked by boat. |
| `TJHH-L5` | 16 Sep 2014 | 18 Sep 2014 | `Snap trap` | 20 | Many traps set on lianas. Checked by boat. |

{% aside type="note" title="Best-supported end dates" %}
The journal never states when Lines 1 and 2 were pulled. Line 1 still caught
animals on 16 September, and Line 2 on 18 September (the final day at camp).
Use those latest documented dates as the end dates and note that in `Notes`.
{% /aside %}

## 2. Create the first event

{% steps %}

1. Open `Events` and select `+`.
2. Choose `TJHH-L1` as `Site ID`.
3. Set `Start Date` to 12 September 2014 and `End Date` to 16 September 2014.
   NAHPU builds the `Event ID` automatically from the Site ID and start date.
4. Leave `Start Time` and `End Time` empty—the journal does not record when the
   line was set or pulled, and an empty field is better than a guess.
5. Set `Primary activity` to `Collecting`.
6. In `Notes`, enter the event notes from the table above and document the
   team's standard convention for overnight capture times:

    ```text
    Capture time 07:00 is the team's standard practice for overnight captures; the exact capture time is unknown.
    ```

    Snap traps and pitfalls were checked in the morning. Writing `07:00` for every overnight
    capture was a team convention that never appeared on paper. Recorded here,
    that explanation travels with every linked specimen so no one mistakes
    `07:00` for an exact capture time.

{% /steps %}

## 3. Add the collection method

{% steps %}

1. Select `Add collection method`.
2. Choose `Snap trap` and set `Count` to `31`.
3. Leave `Brand and Model` and `Size` empty because the paper does not record
   them.

{% /steps %}

Separating methods per event resolves the paper's `Line 1` ambiguity: `TJHH-L1`
holds only snap traps and `TJHH-P1` holds only pitfalls. Any specimen linked to
`TJHH-P1` can only be a pitfall capture, regardless of what was scribbled on the
paper form.

## 4. Add the people

On paper, `Collector` reads `Heru Handika` on every sheet. Yet on 17 September,
Heru stayed in camp preparing specimens while Bima, Citra, Dian, Eko, and Fajar
crossed the lake to check the Pasir Putih lines. Event personnel record who
actually worked each line in each event.

{% steps %}

1. In `Settings` > `Events`, add the personnel roles `Local guide` and
   `Boat operator` (`Leader` and `Helper` are already available).
2. Return to the event, open the personnel tab (people icon), and select
   `Add personnel`.
3. Assign the team members for each event:

    | Event | People and roles |
    | --- | --- |
    | `TJHH-L1`, `TJHH-P1` | Heru Handika (`Leader`); Bima Sampel, Citra Contoh (`Helper`); Eko Pemandu (`Local guide`) |
    | `TJBS-L2` | Bima Sampel (`Leader`); Citra Contoh, Heru Handika (`Helper`) |
    | `TJHH-L3`, `TJHH-P4`, `TJHH-L5` | Heru Handika (`Leader`); Bima Sampel, Citra Contoh, Dian Teladan (`Helper`); Eko Pemandu (`Local guide`); Fajar Nelayan (`Boat operator`) |

{% /steps %}

In [Basic tutorial 5](/en/tutorials/basic/specimens/), each specimen's capture
`Collector` is chosen from its event's personnel, ensuring the collector is
someone who actually worked that line.

Below is the finished event for Line 1. NAHPU built `TJHH-L1-2014-09-12` from
the Site ID and start date, and the start and end times stay empty:

![NAHPU Events page on an iPad for TJHH-L1-2014-09-12, with dates 12 to 16 September 2014, notes that include the 07:00 standard practice, 31 snap traps, and the event personnel](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/event-tjhh-l1.webp)

## 5. Add environmental data

The 13 September journal entry contains the only weather record from the trip:
night temperatures dropping from about 16 °C to 14 °C, no rain, and a sky that
shifted between cloudy and very clear.

{% steps %}

1. In the `TJHH-L1` event, open the environmental data tab (cloud icon).
2. Enter what the journal supports: the temperature range, no rain, and variable
   cloud cover. Note the date in the remarks: `Night of 12–13 September.`
3. Leave the remaining fields empty.

{% /steps %}

## 6. Create the remaining events

{% steps %}

1. With `TJHH-L1` open, choose `Duplicate event` from the three-dot menu. NAHPU
   copies the site, activity, notes, methods, and personnel, advances the dates
   by one day, and creates a blank environmental-data record.
2. Change `Site ID` to `TJHH-P1` and set the dates back to 12–16 September.
3. Replace the collection method with `Pitfall` (count `10`) and update `Notes`.
4. Create the remaining four events the same way using the tables in steps 1
   and 4.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="caution" %}
Review every copied field after duplicating an event. Dates always shift forward
by one day, and a carried-over method or note that does not belong to the new
line is easy to miss.
{% /aside %}

## What you gained over paper

- Trap counts, active dates, and setup notes from the journal are now attached
  directly to the line they describe.
- Each event holds one method, preventing pitfall and snap-trap captures from
  being confused.
- The local guide, boatman, and teammates who checked traps are properly
  credited.
- Data gaps—such as the unrecorded pitfall count on Line 4—remain visible as
  empty fields.

